Q: Press and hold button does not fire onClick A: <p>you should use a boolean to save the current state.</p>

<pre><code>var mouse_is_down = false;
var current_i = 0;              // current_i is used to handle double click (to not act like a hold)

var button = document.querySelector("#myButton");

button.onmousedown = function(){
    mouse_is_down = true;

    // Do thing here for a mousedown event

    setTimeout(
        (function(index){
            return function(){
                if(mouse_is_down &amp;&amp; current_i === index){
                    //do thing when hold                        
                }
            };
        })(++current_i), 500); // time you want to hold before fire action in milliseconds
};

button.onmouseup = function(){
    mouse_is_down = false;
    current_i++;

    // Do thing here for a mouseup event
};
</code></pre>

<p>Fiddle : <a href="http://jsfiddle.net/0bs3omjj/1/" rel="nofollow">link</a></p>

Q: Electron autoUpdater proxy A: <p><code>setProxy()</code> is asynchronous now, so the update check needs to wait until the proxy is actually set.</p>
<p>For example:</p>
<pre><code>const proxyString = &quot;http://127.0.0.1:8080&quot;;

window.webContents.session
  .setProxy({ proxyRules: proxyString })
  .then(() =&gt; {
    console.log(&quot;Proxy configured&quot;);

    return autoUpdater.checkForUpdates();
  })
  .catch((err) =&gt; {
    console.error(&quot;Failed to set proxy or check updates:&quot;, err);
  });
</code></pre>
<p>The important part is calling <code>autoUpdater.checkForUpdates()</code> inside <code>.then()</code>. Otherwise Electron may start the update request before the proxy settings have been applied.</p>
<p>With newer Electron versions, the same thing can also be written with <code>async/await</code>:</p>
<pre><code>async function checkForUpdates() {
  try {
    await window.webContents.session.setProxy({
      proxyRules: &quot;http://127.0.0.1:8080&quot;
    });

    await autoUpdater.checkForUpdates();
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Also note that Electron uses <code>proxyRules</code> as the option name.</p>

Q: Looking for a fast prime-counting function A: <p>I keep thinking about this question and <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/70049343/549617">my original answer</a>, as I'm not quite satisfied with it in that it is quite complex and yet not all that fast as compared to state-of-the-art  Since writing that answer, I've since discovered an implementation that applies Partial Sieving (doing a computation based on the state of the sieve buffer after each individual base prime culling pass) to the Legendre prime counting algorithm.  Partial Sieving has been known since at least 1870 when it was applied by Daniel Friedrich Ernst Meissel to using his algorithm (an augmentation to the Legendre algorithm) to hand calculation of the number of primes to a hundred million (100,000,000) and later to a billion (1,000,000,000); without the use of Partial Sieving, he would not have been able to do especially this second calculation as it otherwise would have required millions of long divisions which he could likely not have completed in his lifetime.  The Meissel algorithm is what is actually used <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/62875264/549617">in Ovinus Real's answer</a> except that he used the conventional recursive calculation for <code>Phi(limit, Pi(cbrt(limit))), where the Pi</code> function is just the determination of the number of primes over the given range, rather than Partial Sieving with the resulting in <code>O(n/((log n)^3)) performance rather than something more like O(n^(2/3)*log log n) when using Partial Sieving.</code></p>
<p>The Legendre prime counting algorithm is even simpler than the Meissel one as it is just <code>Phi(limit, Pi(sqrt(limit))) + Pi(sqrt(limit)) - 1</code>;<code> </code>with Partial Sieving it has an emperical computational complexity of just a little worse than <code>O(limit^(2/3))</code> for smaller limits up to about <code>10^16, which makes it a reasonable answer for use by JavaScript.</code></p>
<p>The following code runs about the same speed on the same CPU as my previous answer but is much simpler, with its main disadvantage that it consumes memory proportional to the square root of the counting range limit - about 800 MegaBytes for the maximum counting limit for JavaScript of <code>2^53-1</code> which is that largest 64-bit float mantissa size used to represent large integers.  If one were to use BigInt's or something similar for larger ranges, the performance would get quite bad due to the extra overheads of composing the large representation from smaller ones.  Enjoy the following code that can be run from within this answer:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="false" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>"use strict";

const MAXVALUE = 9007199254740991; // 2**53 - 1

function *countPrimesTo(limit) {
  if (limit &lt; 3) return limit &lt; 2 ? 0 : 1;
  const sqrtlmt = Math.trunc(Math.sqrt(limit)) &gt;&gt;&gt; 0;
  const sqrtlmtsz = (sqrtlmt + 1) &gt;&gt;&gt; 1;
  const sqrtsqrtlmt = Math.trunc(Math.sqrt(sqrtlmt)) &gt;&gt;&gt; 0;
  const rghs = new Uint32Array(sqrtlmtsz);
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; rghs.length; ++i) rghs[i] = (i + i + 1) &gt;&gt;&gt; 0;
  const phindxs = new Uint32Array(rghs.length);
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; phindxs.length; ++i) phindxs[i] = i &gt;&gt;&gt; 0;
  const phis = new Float64Array(rghs.length);
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; phis.length; ++i) phis[i] =
    Math.trunc(((limit / rghs[i] + 1) / 2)); // tinyPhi2!
  // estimate the total number of operations to be performed;
  // slightly overestimates for smaller limits less than `MAXVALUE`...
  const log10ratio = Math.log10(9007199254740991) - Math.log10(limit);
  const aprxopsratio = Math.pow(4.8, log10ratio);
  const aprxnumops = Math.trunc(13283323268 / aprxopsratio);
  let numops = 0; let numopschk = 100000000
  let nbps = 0; let rsz = rghs.length
  // do the computations for the partial sieving with
  // base primes less than the square root of the square root limit...
  while (rsz &gt; 2 &amp;&amp; rghs[1] &lt;= sqrtsqrtlmt) {
    if (numops &gt;= numopschk) { numopschk += 100000000;
                              yield numops / aprxnumops * 100 }
    // mark all roughs culled in this base prime partial sieving pass...
    const bp = rghs[1];
    rghs[1] = 0 // make phi form by eliminating bp as well!
    for (let cp = bp * bp; cp &lt;= sqrtlmt; cp += bp + bp) { // cull points...
      const cpi = phindxs[cp &gt;&gt; 1] - nbps;
      if (rghs[cpi] == cp) rghs[cpi] = 0; // only if not alreay eliminated...
    }

    let roi = 0; // main inner processing-splitting loop...
    for (let rii = 0; rii &lt; rsz; ++rii) {
      const m = rghs[rii];
      if (m == 0) continue; // skip marked!
      const mbp = m * bp // odd product due to all roughs are odd!

      // crutial pis list updating based on size of `mbp` whether
      // less than or equal to or above `sqrtlmt`...
      phis[roi] = phis[rii] - ( mbp &lt;= sqrtlmt ? phis[phindxs[mbp &gt;&gt;&gt; 1] - nbps]
                                // add one to make it phi form!...
                                : phindxs[(limit / mbp - 1) &gt;&gt;&gt; 1] - nbps + 1 )
      rghs[roi] = m; roi += 1; // eliminate culled roughs and advance out index!
    }

    // update phindxs list to reflect new state of roughs; the only remaining
    // valid phindx values are those corresponding to current roughs...
    let ci = sqrtlmtsz - 1;
    for (let cp = (Math.trunc(sqrtlmt / bp - 1) | 1) &gt;&gt;&gt; 0; cp &gt;= bp; cp -= 2) {
      const c = phindxs[cp &gt;&gt;&gt; 1] - nbps; // cp always odd; correction offset!
      const endi = (cp * bp) &gt;&gt;&gt; 1;
      for (; ci &gt;= endi; --ci) phindxs[ci] -= c;
    }
    // get current number of operations...
    const mxcnti = phindxs[Math.trunc(sqrtlmt / bp - 1) &gt;&gt;&gt; 1];
    numops += phindxs[phindxs.length - 1] - mxcnti;

    rsz = roi; nbps += 1; // update loop monitor variables!
  }

  let phi = phis[0]; // accumulate result from pis...
  for (let i = 1; i &lt; rsz; ++i) phi -= phis[i];

  // computation of the Unique Prime Pairs (UPP's) as all roughs are primes...
  // add all the "one"'s pairs calc may need!...
  phi += Math.trunc((rsz - 2) * (rsz - 1) / 2);
  for (let p1i = 1; p1i &lt; rsz - 1; ++p1i) { // never gets to limit!
    if (numops &gt;= numopschk) { numopschk += 100000000;
                              yield numops / aprxnumops * 100 }
    const p1 = rghs[p1i];
    const qp1 = limit / p1; const endi = phindxs[(qp1 / p1 - 1) &gt;&gt;&gt; 1] - nbps;
    if (endi &lt;= p1i) break; // break here when no more pairs!
    for (let p2i = p1i + 1; p2i &lt;= endi; ++p2i)
      phi += phindxs[(qp1 / rghs[p2i] - 1) &gt;&gt;&gt; 1];
    phi -= (endi - p1i) * (nbps + p1i - 1) // efficient to remove what's not needed!
    numops += endi - p1i;
  }
  return phi + nbps + rsz - 1; // the adjustment for one already done!
}

let cancelled = false;

function doit() {
  const limit =  Math.floor(parseFloat(document.getElementById('limit').value));
  const start = Date.now();
  const prgrsgen = countPrimesTo(limit);
  function prgrsfnc() {
    if (cancelled) {
      document.getElementById('output').innerText = "Cancelled!!!";
    }
    else {
      const prgrs = prgrsgen.next();
      if (!prgrs.done) { // pgrslt.value is a percent done...            
        document.getElementById('output').innerText =
          "Counted " + (prgrs.value.toFixed(3)) + "%";
        setTimeout(prgrsfnc, 0); return;
      }
      // done; value is the count result...
      const elpsd = Date.now() - start;
      document.getElementById('output').innerText =
        "Found " + prgrs.value + " primes up to "
          + limit + " in " + elpsd + " milliseconds.";
    }
    cancelled = false;    
    document.getElementById('go').onclick = strtclick;
    document.getElementById('go').value = "Start Counting...";            
    document.getElementById('go').disabled = false;
    return;
  }
  prgrsfnc();
}

const cancelclick = function () {
  cancelled = true;
  document.getElementById('go').disabled = true;
  document.getElementById('go').value = "Cancelled!!!";
  document.getElementById('go').onclick = strtclick;
}

const strtclick = function () {
  const limit =  Math.floor(parseFloat(document.getElementById('limit').value));
  if (!Number.isInteger(limit) || (limit &lt; 0) || (limit &gt; MAXVALUE)) {    
    document.getElementById('output').innerText =
      "Top limit must be an integer between 0 and " + MAXVALUE + "!";
    return;
  }
  document.getElementById('output').innerText = "Counted 0.000%";
  document.getElementById('go').onclick = cancelclick;
  document.getElementById('go').value = "Running, click to cancel...";
  cancelled = false;
  setTimeout(doit, 0);
};

document.getElementById('go').onclick = strtclick;</code></pre>
