</p>
<p>On my AMD 7840HS CPU at 5.1 GHz (single threaded boost), this takes about 58 seconds to find the number of primes to the maximum limit, which takes about 69 seconds with my original answer to run to this same limit; these tests were run with Google Chrome version 149.0.7827.200 (Official Build) (64-bit).  So this answer is a little bit faster than the original answer in spite of more operations due to much less dependence on sieving; as mentioned in the original answer, a better sieve would likely make it about twice as fast.</p>
<p>The Legendre and the Meissel Prime Counting Algorithms are two extremes of the problem, with the Legendre algorithm doing sieving only up to the square root of the counting range limit but many more operations and the Meissel algorithm sieving to the limit to the two thirds power with only a fraction of the number of operations.  From 1985 to <a href="https://t5k.org/nthprime/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Xavier Gourdon's paper of 2001</a> there have been a succession of algorithm refinements that actually are neither one nor the other that allow one to tune the ratio between the amount of sieving and the amount of other computation while at the same time using some mathematical refinements to also further reduce the number of operations somewhat.  Using Gourdon's algorithm with a reasonably fast sieve makes sieving no longer the bottleneck as for the Meissel algorithm and refinements in the implementation can keep the time to count across the sieving buffer not much more than the execution cost of the divisions for each of the operations.</p>
<p>I suspect that implementing this algorithm in JavaScript would reduce the execution time by about a factor of five or more yet would still greatly reduce the required RAM memory although it would be several times more complex than the above Legendre Parial Sieving implementation.  I may submit yet another answer to this thread implementing the Gourdon Prime Counting Algorithm in JavaScript.</p>
